### What is the Factory Method pattern?

The Factory Method pattern defines an interface for creating an object but lets a dedicated factory decide which concrete class to instantiate based on input. The caller asks for a product by type and receives a ready-to-use object without knowing its concrete class.

In banking terms, when Rajesh needs to process a payment, he does not construct a `UpiPayment` or a `CardPayment` directly. He calls the factory with a type code and gets back a `PaymentMethod` that is ready to use.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface PaymentMethod {
    void pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount);
    String type();
}
```

### Why does the Factory Method pattern exist?

Without a factory, creation logic is scattered across the codebase. Every place that needs a payment method would have its own `if-else` or `switch` deciding which class to instantiate. This violates the Open/Closed Principle because adding a new payment type means editing every call site.

The Factory Method solves this by centralising the decision in one place. It satisfies Dependency Inversion because callers depend on the `PaymentMethod` interface, not on concrete classes. It satisfies Open/Closed because adding a new type means writing one new class and adding one case in the factory.

```java
// Without factory: scattered creation logic (bad)
PaymentMethod pm;
if (type.equals("UPI")) {
    pm = new UpiPayment();
} else if (type.equals("CARD")) {
    pm = new CardPayment();
}
// This code is duplicated everywhere a payment is created
```

### How does the Factory Method work internally?

The factory holds the mapping from type codes to concrete classes. When `create` is called, it looks up the type, instantiates the matching product, and returns it through the product interface. The caller never sees the concrete class.

```java
package com.bank.factory;

import com.bank.service.PaymentMethod;
import com.bank.service.UpiPayment;
import com.bank.service.CardPayment;
import com.bank.service.NetBankingPayment;

public class PaymentFactory {

    public static PaymentMethod create(String type) {
        return switch (type.toUpperCase()) {
            case "UPI"  -> new UpiPayment();
            case "CARD" -> new CardPayment();
            case "NETBANKING" -> new NetBankingPayment();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Unknown payment type: " + type);
        };
    }
}
```

### How do you implement concrete products?

Each concrete product implements the `PaymentMethod` interface and contains its own processing logic. The products are fully independent, so a bug in UPI logic cannot affect card logic.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class UpiPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(UpiPayment.class.getName());

    @Override
    public void pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        log.info("Processing UPI payment of INR " + amount
                 + " for account " + accountId);
        // UPI-specific logic: generate VPA, call NPCI gateway
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "UPI"; }
}

public class CardPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(CardPayment.class.getName());

    @Override
    public void pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        log.info("Processing card payment of INR " + amount
                 + " for account " + accountId);
        // Card-specific logic: tokenise card, call acquirer
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "CARD"; }
}

public class NetBankingPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(NetBankingPayment.class.getName());

    @Override
    public void pay(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        log.info("Processing net-banking payment of INR " + amount
                 + " for account " + accountId);
        // Net-banking-specific logic: redirect to bank portal
    }

    @Override
    public String type() { return "NETBANKING"; }
}
```

### How is Factory Method different from Abstract Factory?

Factory Method creates one product at a time based on a type code. Abstract Factory creates families of related products without specifying their concrete classes.

| Aspect | Factory Method | Abstract Factory |
|---|---|---|
| Creates | One product at a time | Family of related products |
| Input | Type code or parameter | Factory object selection |
| Structure | Single factory class or method | Interface with multiple create methods |
| Use case | Pick one payment method | Create a matched set (UI theme: button + textbox + dialog) |
| Complexity | Low | Higher |

```java
// Factory Method: one product
PaymentMethod pm = PaymentFactory.create("UPI");

// Abstract Factory: family of related products
BankingUIFactory factory = new DarkThemeFactory();
Button btn = factory.createButton();
TextField field = factory.createTextField();
```

### What are common mistakes with the Factory Method?

The most common mistake is returning a concrete type instead of the interface, which defeats the purpose of the pattern.

```java
// Wrong: caller depends on the concrete class
public static UpiPayment create(String type) {
    return new UpiPayment(); // caller is coupled to UpiPayment
}

// Correct: caller depends only on the interface
public static PaymentMethod create(String type) {
    return new UpiPayment(); // caller sees PaymentMethod
}
```

Another mistake is forgetting to handle unknown types, which causes silent failures in production.

```java
// Wrong: returns null for unknown type
case default -> null; // caller gets NullPointerException later

// Correct: throws immediately with a clear message
case default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(
    "Unknown payment type: " + type);
```

### How do you use the Factory Method with Spring?

In Spring, the cleanest approach is injecting a `Map<String, PaymentMethod>` where Spring populates the map from all beans implementing the interface. The map key is the bean name.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

@Component
public class PaymentService {

    private final Map<String, PaymentMethod> strategies;

    public PaymentService(Map<String, PaymentMethod> strategies) {
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    public void processPayment(String type, String accountId,
                               BigDecimal amount) {
        PaymentMethod method = strategies.get(type.toUpperCase());
        if (method == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "No payment method registered for: " + type);
        }
        method.pay(accountId, amount);
    }
}
```

Adding a new payment type is just writing a new `@Component` class. Spring adds it to the map automatically with zero code changes to `PaymentService`.

### When should you use the Factory Method?

Use it when the caller should not know or care which concrete class it gets, when creation logic involves configuration or dependency wiring that should be hidden, or when you expect new product types to be added over time.

Do not use it when there is only one possible product and there is no prospect of more, because the factory adds indirection for no benefit.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.factory.PaymentFactory;
import com.bank.service.PaymentMethod;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PaymentDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PaymentMethod upi = PaymentFactory.create("UPI");
        upi.pay("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));

        PaymentMethod card = PaymentFactory.create("CARD");
        card.pay("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("12500.00"));
    }
}
```

```text
Processing UPI payment of INR 5000.00 for account SAV2001
Processing card payment of INR 12500.00 for account SAV2001
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The senior distinction is between three related patterns. The **simple parameterised factory** is a single class with a static method that takes a type code, which is what most interviewers mean and what production code uses for payment methods. The **formal Factory Method** is a design where subclasses override a creation method, so each subclass produces a different product. The **Abstract Factory** produces whole families of related objects.

In production, the factory is also the right place to wire dependencies, read configuration, and apply caching. For example, if constructing a `CardPayment` requires an API key from configuration, that complexity is hidden inside the factory rather than exposed to every caller.

```java
// Formal Factory Method: subclasses override creation
public abstract class TransactionProcessor {
    public void process(BigDecimal amount) {
        PaymentMethod pm = createPaymentMethod(); // subclass decides
        pm.pay("SAV2001", amount);
    }
    protected abstract PaymentMethod createPaymentMethod();
}

public class UpiProcessor extends TransactionProcessor {
    @Override
    protected PaymentMethod createPaymentMethod() {
        return new UpiPayment();
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does the Factory Method satisfy the Open/Closed Principle? Adding a new product is a new class plus one factory case, with no changes to callers.
- Can you combine Factory Method with Strategy? Yes, the factory selects the strategy at runtime.
- How do you handle unknown types? Always throw a clear exception rather than returning null.
- What is the difference between a factory and a constructor? A factory can return any subtype, cache instances, and hide creation complexity, while a constructor always creates a new instance of exactly one class.
